From b13dd80f3242e01fe84ce03fbb408ac60ecf6036 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 04:53:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 34/41] aout: correct drift with a PI controller, bounded in
 cents

The correction was a relay: step the rate until the drift halved, then
reverse. It could only stop by driving the rate back through zero, so it
hunted around the set point and could never hold the standing offset a
device off nominal rate needs. A PI controller's integral holds it.

Bound it in cents rather than permille of the rate: resampling corrects
drift by changing pitch, so how far out of tune it may go is the question.
Cents being logarithmic, the bound stays an interval throughout - clamping
the rate offset would allow more detuning down than up.

aout_FiltersSetDetune() returns what it managed, the request having been
rounded to whole Hz and possibly clamped, and the difference is fed back;
integrating against the request would wind up at the bound.

The correction now lasts the life of playback, so aout_StopResampling()
goes: it describes the device, which a flush and a pause do not move.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
